{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal changes the fixed-point carrier from node labels to finite sets of support trees, and defines the monotone operator on the lattice of tree sets. It states polynomiality holds when tree depth is bounded by node count and membership is decided by dynamic programming over a DAG of warrants. However, the full fixed-point iteration operates over the lattice of tree sets, and the proposal itself admits this lattice has exponential width. Bounding tree depth alone does not bound the number of distinct support trees: the number of trees with depth at most |N| can still be exponential in |N|, and a monotone operator over an exponentially-wide lattice may require exponentially many iterations to reach the fixed point. The proposed dynamic-programming membership check only decides whether a single candidate tree is a valid support tree; it does not bound the cost of computing the full fixed point, because one may need to discover and combine exponentially many canonical tree representatives. The commitment to polynomial cost is therefore not met by the argument given — the target fails the problem's requirement that polynomiality be preserved.","target":"eb284b9c58482802a649b5199fa4c98782810d0fb2e3ea21b9c0ab84b6f1191e"}]}